## Step 3: Controller Integration

```java
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

@RestController
public class IntegrationController {

    private final TaskService taskService;

    public IntegrationController(TaskService taskService) {
        this.taskService = taskService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/execute-pipeline")
    public CompletableFuture<Integer> runPipeline() {
        return taskService.fetchExternalData("https://example.com")
                .thenCompose(apiData -> taskService.processHeavyData(apiData));
    }
}
```

---

## Key Design Points

**Virtual Threads (I/O Tasks)**
- One executor per task spawned
- No thread pool limits
- Ideal for API calls, database queries, blocking I/O
- Low CPU overhead, high concurrency

**Platform Thread Pool (CPU Tasks)**
- Core pool size = number of CPU cores
- Prevents context-switching overhead
- Ideal for intensive calculations, encryption, parsing
- Bounded concurrency matches hardware capacity

**Pipeline Composition**
- Use `thenCompose()` to chain async results
- I/O data flows directly into CPU processing
- Both executors work independently without blocking
